      "writtenOn": "2026-04-29T15:33:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "```\n/* Ensure we are not just casting pointers blindly if alignment matters */\negis_api_return_t ret \u003d egis_finger_match(\n    templ, \n    templ_count, \n    (FP_EGIS_IMAGE *)image, // Add a static_assert here to verify size/alignment if possible\n    match_index,\n    template_update ? update_bitmap : NULL\n);\n```",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 204,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 206,
        "endChar": 42
      },
      "revId": "d88db1fba163aca7fb6aee481250a27e2a450f3a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bfa9fa09_fc86bc51",
        "filename": "driver/fingerprint/egis/egis_private.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 206,
      "author": {
        "id": 3698372
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-30T04:24:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The FP APIs is designed to use a uint8_t * to transfer image data. However, at the moment there is no pure raw image format being supported. As a result, a forced cast has to exist somewhere in the pipeline.\nIn general, a safer implementation should validate the image type or buffer size before performing any casting.\nThat said, I believe the original design assumed the data would always be pure image data.\nBecause of that assumption, most of the APIs do not include an explicit size argument.\nGiven these constraints, I don’t think there is a fully robust way to prevent this issue without changing the interface itself.",
      "parentUuid": "83b28082_aa634d83",
